[metadata]
title: Creality Pika – The New Budget King of 3D Scanners for Reverse Engineering
channel: @octopusinc_
duration: 24:38
source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eE_AkTjT908
upload_date: 20260723
transcript_source: captions+frames
route: visual

---

[00:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hey everyone, this is the brand new Creality&nbsp; Pika. It\'s one of the smallest and lightest&nbsp;&nbsp; laser scanners out there, weighing only 256 g. It&nbsp; comes with a pair of depth cameras. The resolution&nbsp;&nbsp; is a bit lower than the Raptor series. It also&nbsp; has blue and white LED to help with both laser&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scanning and capturing color texture. Here\'s the&nbsp; infrared projector, laser projector and the RGB&nbsp;&nbsp; camera. The stereo depth camera are positioned&nbsp; very close together to cover the same capture&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'area as the Raptor series. They need to use&nbsp; wide-angle lenses. On the top, you find the power&nbsp;&nbsp; button along with a start stop button. This is the&nbsp; air outlet port and the fan runs continuously. A&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quarter inch thread in the bottom. And here&nbsp; is a USB C port. And there is a switch that&nbsp;&nbsp; lets you toggle between USB cable mode and Wi-Fi&nbsp; mode. And here is the battery compartment. The&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pika comes with two batteries and has it own USB C&nbsp;charging port, though the charger isn\'t included.&nbsp;&nbsp; The positive and negative terminals are placed&nbsp; on the same side, so be careful to avoid any&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'metal contact. The battery has an LED charging&nbsp; indicator. It glows red while charging and turns&nbsp;&nbsp; green once it\'s fully charged. It\'s got pretty&nbsp; solid battery life. I managed to get more than&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'five scans out of it during my test. It also comes&nbsp; bundle with six and 10 mm reflective markers.&nbsp;&nbsp; And here\'s the ND filter, which lets you scan in&nbsp; daylight. I won\'t be using it in this test. And&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s the mini tripod, which honestly you will&nbsp; probably never use it. At the bottom of the box,&nbsp;&nbsp; you\'ll find a glass calibration board. Since&nbsp; Pika is a true laser scanner, is calibrated to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the known distances on this board using the same&nbsp; reflective markers you use for scanning. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; make sure you don\'t lose it, scratch it, or&nbsp; break it. Here\'s the 2meter USB C cable. And&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s the included turntable. I don\'t recommend&nbsp; it for long-term use, but it\'s handy for getting&nbsp;&nbsp; started with your first scan. In this test, I&nbsp; will be evaluating this 3D scanner as a measuring&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tool for reverse engineering application. I\'ll&nbsp; focus on three key features. precision, accuracy,&nbsp;&nbsp; and resolution. We will see if the Pika can&nbsp; be used professionally for reverse engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automotive parts intended for 3D printing. You&nbsp; can connect the Pika to your PC either through&nbsp;&nbsp; its Wi-Fi hotspot or with a USB C cable. On Android&nbsp; phones, you can only connect via Wi-Fi. Before you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start using the Pika, you need to calibrate&nbsp; the scanner. Calibration can be done either&nbsp;&nbsp; by connecting to an Android phone or to a PC.&nbsp; In this demo, I will show the PC method which&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'require downloading and installing the Creality&nbsp; scan software. To calibrate using a PC, you will&nbsp;&nbsp; need to connect the scanner with the USB C cable.&nbsp; If you are using a desktop PC, plug the USB C cable&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into one of the rear ports for a more stable&nbsp; connection. Once you plug it in, the software&nbsp;&nbsp; will show the scanner as connected in the upper&nbsp; left corner of the screen. From there, just click&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the calibration button. With the Pika, you will&nbsp; need to manually enter the calibration board serial&nbsp;&nbsp; number which is printed on the back of the board.&nbsp; Your computer must be connected to the internet so&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it can fetch the correction data from Creality&nbsp; server. Then click on okay. All you need to do is&nbsp;&nbsp; align the scanner with the yellow box and the&nbsp; software will guide you on where to move. The&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'calibration process is straightforward and among&nbsp; the easiest to perform. The Pika is designed for&nbsp;&nbsp; wireless use. So I will switch to wireless mode&nbsp; for this test. The Wi-Fi hotspot password is shown on this page'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In Creality scan click the Wi-Fi refresh button. It will&nbsp; detect the Pika hotspot. Select it and click&nbsp;&nbsp; connect. On first use, you will need to enter&nbsp; the password. And make sure your desktop isn\'t&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connected to a LAN network, otherwise the&nbsp; wireless connection will fail. Let\'s begin&nbsp;&nbsp; with the first test scanner resolution. Click&nbsp; on new project and enter a project name. This&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will take you to the project page. I\'ll set&nbsp; the resolution to 0.1 mm which is the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp; available for a laser line scanner equal to the&nbsp; width of the laser line itself. With the Pika,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you need to move the scanner in and out during&nbsp; initialization. Once it first detects the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can use the preview screen to observe the&nbsp; laser line and adjust the capture range. Ignore&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the range scale bar on the left and focus instead&nbsp; on the seven laser line stripes in the preview&nbsp;&nbsp; screen. Move the scanner closer or further&nbsp; until the lines appear clearly on the surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Initializing Pika is a bit trickier&nbsp; than with the Raptor or Sermoon series,&nbsp;&nbsp; but it becomes straightforward once you get&nbsp; used to it. I flip the owl to scan the other&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'side. This will create a separate scan which I&nbsp; will later align with the first one. Now that&nbsp;&nbsp; I\'m familiar with Pika, the second scan&nbsp; locks onto the surface much more quickly.'

[06:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Click on the first scan and then&nbsp; select the clipping plane. Choose&nbsp;&nbsp; three marker dots and the turntable will&nbsp; be automatically removed. Alternatively,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can use the connected component function.&nbsp; Invert the selection and remove the unwanted&nbsp;&nbsp; data. Hold the control key, select both scans,&nbsp; then select fusion. Set the resolution to 0.1 mm,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same value I used during scanning. Then adjust&nbsp; noise removal to 50%. After fusion, some leftover&nbsp;&nbsp; point cloud data may remain and use the lasso tool&nbsp; or the single body selection tool to remove it.'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'After removing uh don\'t forget to click on&nbsp; save data. Next, click on the alignment tool.&nbsp;&nbsp; I\'ll use automatic mode and then click on&nbsp; the preview. If there are enough feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the software will automatically align them. Then&nbsp; click on apply because these are two separate&nbsp;&nbsp; meshes aligned together. You will need to run&nbsp; the mesh function before exporting. This step&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'eliminates seam line noise and smoothly welds&nbsp; the scans into a single model. I will export&nbsp;&nbsp; this model in OBJ format for comparison. Here\'s my&nbsp; collection of owl scans that I have created over&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the past two years. Move to the first pair,&nbsp; Pika and EinStar 2. From what I see here,&nbsp;&nbsp; the resolution is comparable at 0.1 mm.&nbsp; Okay, next let\'s move to the Metro X.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The Matro X has much lower resolution than the&nbsp; Pika. Let\'s move to the next one. The 3D Maker&nbsp;&nbsp; Pro Moose. Pika also has better resolution&nbsp; than the Moose in this test. Let\'s move to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the next one. Einstar Vega. The resolution&nbsp; is very close between Pika and the Vega. Even when I check on the back side, I think&nbsp; the Pika is very close to the Vega in term&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of resolution. Ok Next the MAF tree. Okay, it\'s&nbsp; clearly that MAF 3 can produce a higher resolution&nbsp;&nbsp; than the Pika. Next, let\'s compare with the Sermoon&nbsp; P1. In the front, they look very very close. But&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the back side, I can see that the P1 has&nbsp; higher resolution than the Pika. Okay. Next,&nbsp;&nbsp; the X1 pretty much the same. When looking from&nbsp; the front, but looking from the back side, the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'X1 produce a higher resolution than the Pika. And&nbsp; let\'s move to the S1. Yes, very close when looking&nbsp;&nbsp; from the front. This one has better resolution&nbsp; when looking from the back side. Next the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Raptor. Very similar at the front. The Raptor&nbsp; produce higher resolution when looking from the&nbsp;&nbsp; back. Okay. Next the Otter, the Pika has higher resolution&nbsp; than the Otter in a small mode. I remember I fused&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Otter with 0.15 mm. Okay. The next the last&nbsp; pair the Otter lite, Pika can produce much higher&nbsp;&nbsp; resolution than the Otter Lite in a small mode.&nbsp; The next I will test precision by scanning this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'egg in three different orientations. In wireless&nbsp; mode Pika mirror the feed onto its 3 inc LCD screen. Since the&nbsp;&nbsp; display is small I prefer monitoring on my larger&nbsp; desktop monitor. And here the scan on the second&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'orientation. And this is the final alignment. The&nbsp; egg isn\'t perfectly round. It has about 50 micron&nbsp;&nbsp; of natural out of roundness. Since 50 micron is&nbsp; the threshold for a methology grade 3D scanner,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detecting this slight deviation allows the scanner&nbsp; to correctly align each scan even when the object&nbsp;&nbsp; appears round and symmetrical. Here\'s the three&nbsp; scans of the egg shell. I\'ll use the automatic&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'alignment function. The Pika can align the&nbsp; eggshell with its 50 micron deviation seamlessly,&nbsp;&nbsp; leaving no visible seam lines on par with the&nbsp; Raptor and Sermoon series. A 3D scanner that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fails this test will struggle to align multiple&nbsp; scans. It will also be unable to properly align&nbsp;&nbsp; thin shell objects which are widely used in&nbsp; automotive parts. This eggshell was also scanned&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with picker using an Android mobile app. As you&nbsp; can see, it produced a seam lines for critical&nbsp;&nbsp; precision. Connect Pika to a PC for the best&nbsp; result. The seam line was generated because the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scan data of the same egg shell contains slight&nbsp; deviations. And here when connected to the PC,&nbsp;&nbsp; the precision is greatly improved. Next, I will&nbsp; test accuracy using class one gauge block. Uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this block is 100 mm long with only 0.3 micron&nbsp; of error. That\'s smaller than a single bacteria.&nbsp;&nbsp; Since my room temperature is 7° C over 20, I will&nbsp; need to add eight micron for thermal expansion.'

[12:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In BricsCAD, I trace a line along one side and&nbsp; offset it by 100 mm plus 8 microns. The Pika scan&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data comes out 30 to 40 micron too long, but the&nbsp; accuracy error still falls within the 50 micron range.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that qualify it as a methology grade 3D scanner&nbsp; on par with the Raptor and Sermoon series. Next,&nbsp;&nbsp; I\'ll test the angular precision. Uh, this is also&nbsp; a class one 90° angle. I position it upright so I&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could scan both sides without needing alignment.&nbsp; In BricsCAD, the 90° angle reads as 89.8°.&nbsp;&nbsp; which translate to 0.2 of angular error.&nbsp; That\'s considerably higher than the Raptor&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and Sermoon series which stay under 0.02°. Next,&nbsp; I\'ll test scanning with color texture. To scan&nbsp;&nbsp; with color texture, select global marker&nbsp; and then toggle on color mapping. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you will scan the markers together with the&nbsp; RGB color camera. On the upper right corner,&nbsp;&nbsp; you will see the RGB camera\'s preview screen and&nbsp; use that for framing the scan. Once finished,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'click on point cloud. You would then&nbsp; capture the point cloud just like you&nbsp;&nbsp; normally do. For the size of this object,&nbsp; I will use 0.2 mm for the resolution.'

[14:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'After fusing the point cloud, you will need&nbsp; to generate a mesh in order to map the color&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'texture. In the mesh processing tab, there is a&nbsp; hole filling function that automatically close&nbsp;&nbsp; a small gaps not capture on the other side.&nbsp; After hole filling, I will apply the color&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mapping. I then export it as an OBJ file&nbsp; which includes the color texture. And here&nbsp;&nbsp; is the result. The resolution is on par with&nbsp; the Raptor series. The color mapping is highly&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'accurate and processes quickly with the color&nbsp; tones also coming out true to the original. Next, I will test the infrared mode, which&nbsp; allows scanning without markers. For this helmet,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I will select medium size and scan&nbsp; in geometry mode. In infrared mode,&nbsp;&nbsp; it captures at 30 frame per second. The tracking&nbsp; performance on this helmet is comparable to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the NIR system found in the Raptor and Sermoon&nbsp; series. I would set 0.25 for the resolution. The NIR result is on par with the&nbsp; Raptor and Sermoon series. Next,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I test tracking performance in laser mode.&nbsp; I\'ll begin by scanning the markers on both&nbsp;&nbsp; sides using the global marker scanning function.&nbsp; The scanner needs a deep capture range to track&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'markers from one side to the other. Let\'s see if&nbsp; the Pika can track from one side to another. Okay, the Pika can track. So the tracking&nbsp; performance is on par with the Raptor Pro,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Raptor X, and the Sermoon series. The Einstar&nbsp; 2 can also do this, but the Raptor original&nbsp;&nbsp; cannot track this also with the Rivo Point Metro&nbsp; series. Next, I can scan for the point cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I scan on this side over the turntable. And&nbsp; I stop the scan and remove the turntable. Press on continue scanning. And now it\'s&nbsp; ready to scan the opposite side. Uh since&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the markers have already been registered, the&nbsp; scanner will automatically detect and align&nbsp;&nbsp; the scan data. Uh this is how you scan&nbsp; the thin shell object in a single pass.'

[17:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'When meshing the scan, I check the triangle&nbsp; count here and set it to roughly the same&nbsp;&nbsp; value. Now I\'m running the hole filling&nbsp; function and here is the end result.'

[17:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Next, let\'s move on to scanning and automative&nbsp; part, the speaker trim. Now it\'s time to switch&nbsp;&nbsp; out the batteries. For the part about this&nbsp; side, I set the resolution to 0.2 mm. It&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'might be a bit late to mention, but the Pika&nbsp; has no internal computer. It can\'t operate&nbsp;&nbsp; independently or store data. You need to connect&nbsp; it to a PC or Android phone. In a wireless mode,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m getting 60 frame per second on this PC&nbsp; while the Android phone delivers about 40 fps. Now I\'m flipping to scan the other side.'

[18:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now I\'m using the plane clipping function to&nbsp; select three markers and remove the turntable.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I do the same on the first scan. I set the&nbsp; resolution to 0.15 mm and noise removal to 50%. Next, I will align the scan data&nbsp; using the manual alignment. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'align the fixed scan to match the floating scan.&nbsp; Then use your mouse to click for the pair points.&nbsp;&nbsp; It doesn\'t has to be precise. Three pair points&nbsp; is usually enough. Here\'s the alignment result.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Scanners that pass the egg shell test typically&nbsp; have no issues aligning a thin shell automotive&nbsp;&nbsp; part. If you can scan and align both sides,&nbsp; you can confidently reverse engineer the part.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There are very few consumer grade 3D scanners&nbsp; that can actually achieve this. Surprisingly, the&nbsp;&nbsp; $629 Creality Pika can handle this as well. And&nbsp; next, let\'s try the Pika with an Android phone. Uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'after powering the scanner, connect your phone&nbsp; to its Wi-Fi hotspot and disable mobile data.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then launch the Creality scan mobile app. I\'m&nbsp; scanning this in NIR mode with no markers. The tracking performance is good and on&nbsp; par with the Raptor and Sermoon series.'

[20:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For the capture range, I set it to a medium mode. Now, let\'s try to scan this&nbsp; motorcycle seat with the large mode.'

[20:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'To import the scan data to your PC,&nbsp; make sure both the phone and the PC&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are connected to the same Wi-Fi&nbsp; network. Tap the export button in&nbsp;&nbsp; the mobile app and it will prompt&nbsp; you to scan a QR code on your PC.'

[21:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now everything is export to your PC and then&nbsp; you can fuse the point cloud as you normally&nbsp;&nbsp; do. And here is the result. It looks very similar&nbsp; to the result from the NIR from Raptor Sermoon series&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and also the Otter. In large mode the Pika&nbsp; capture range is much smaller than the Otter&nbsp;&nbsp; and Otter Lite. It can\'t cover the entire&nbsp; scooter, but it works fine for the seat.'

[21:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s move to the conclusion. Uh this is not a&nbsp; paid review. I have never earned any commission&nbsp;&nbsp; from the product I test even though I have&nbsp; produced over 200 Creality related videos and&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[22:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'no money has ever been exchanged. The Pika is&nbsp; also the last review Creality will provide as&nbsp;&nbsp; uh Creality has ended support for this channel.&nbsp; Looking at the pricing, the Pika comes in lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[22:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a pre-launch price of $629. In term of&nbsp; resolution, it\'s slightly lower than the Raptor&nbsp;&nbsp; and Sermoon series. In my opinion, the resolution&nbsp; is acceptable for most automotive parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[22:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but when scanning small coins or jewelry, the&nbsp; difference in resolution becomes noticeable. The&nbsp;&nbsp; Pika delivers excellent precision seamlessly&nbsp; aligned the multiple scans and essential&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[22:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capability for reverse engineering. For accuracy,&nbsp; the dimensional error is under 50 micron on par&nbsp;&nbsp; with the Raptor and Sermoon series. However, the&nbsp; angular accuracy error is noticeably higher.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[23:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The Pika NIR performance is on par with the&nbsp; Raptor, but it doesn\'t match the Otter when&nbsp;&nbsp; it comes to face or headshot scanning. Thanks&nbsp; to it high precision, the Pika can easily align&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[23:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multiple scans, even a thin shell parts that&nbsp; most scanner fail to register. It also offer&nbsp;&nbsp; wireless integration that works seamlessly out&nbsp; of the box with no additional cost. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[23:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the tracking performance is better than the Raptor&nbsp; and on par with the Raptor Pro. The Raptor was my&nbsp;&nbsp; budget pick for reverse engineering, but after 2&nbsp; years, it\'s been overtaken with its lower price&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[24:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and built-in wireless. The Pika is now my&nbsp; top recommendation in the budget category. If&nbsp;&nbsp; you are interested in learning how to reverse&nbsp; engineering automotive parts for 3D printing,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[24:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I have created a course on Ko Fi and we\'ll&nbsp; continue adding tutorials along the way. The&nbsp;&nbsp; link is in the description. All right, thanks&nbsp; you guys for watching. I see you in the next video.'
